  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page0.0
    value: Preface
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page0.1
    value: "\tListen carefully, apprentice. A safe is not your enemy. It is your
      interlocutor. It speaks to you in the language of clicks, and only one who
      knows how to stay silent and listen will hear its sounds. \n\tForget haste.
      \n\tForget force. \n\tHere, patience wins."
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page0.2
    value: "\tFrom the notes of a master locksmith, left for those who follow in
      his footsteps"
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page0.3
    value: Chapter I. On the Nature of the Lock
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page0.4
    value: Chapter II. Preparation
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page0.5
    value: "\tEvery safe is a vault of secrets, and its secret lies in the combination.
      Three numbers, four, or even five \u2014 each hidden behind a turn of the dial.
      The mechanism is built so that as it turns, it emits a soft click at every
      position. That is its breathing. Count these sounds, for they are the steps
      of your journey.\n\tBut among them are special sounds. The ones that ring slightly
      differently \u2014 louder. Train your ear, for it is your only instrument.\n\tAnd
      should you err \u2014 the safe will fall silent and reset everything you have
      done. Then begin again. Such is its nature."
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page0.6
    value: "\tBefore you touch the dial, create silence around yourself.\n\tTurn
      the music down \u2014 it is your enemy.\n\tBreathe evenly. If your hand trembles,
      you will falter.\n\tEvery lock has its own pulse. Listen for its beating to
      learn its secret."
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page1.0
    value: Chapter III. The Art of Listening
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page1.1
    value: "\tFirst pass: clockwise\n\tBegin turning the dial clockwise. Slowly.
      Attentively. Listen.\n\tMost steps sound the same \u2014 even, dry clicks.
      But somewhere along the circle a different sound will come. A special one.
      The very one. Remember the number at which it sounded.\n\tIf there is no special
      click \u2014 it means the correct number is not in this direction. But it will
      be. Keep listening.\n\tSecond pass: counterclockwise\n\tNow begin turning counterclockwise.
      Listen again. Search again for the special click. Remember the number at which
      it sounded.\n\tReturn to the start. Begin slowly, moving toward the first number.
      Step by step. Click by click. Draw near to the place where you heard the special
      sound.\n\tIf you found that very click \u2014 the combination is correct. Release
      the lock.\n\tAnd repeat until the safe opens."
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page1.2
    value: Chapter IV. Working the Dial
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page1.3
    value: "1) Grip the lock and rotate around the center of the dial. The dial will
      begin to turn.\n2) Listen to the clicks. Each click is one step, one position.
      But remember: there is only one special click at each step. Only at the correct
      number. Only in the correct direction.\n3) Lead the dial in the required direction.
      If a step calls for clockwise rotation \u2014 turn clockwise. Counterclockwise
      \u2014 turn the other way. The mechanism remembers the direction of the last
      rotation.\n4) Stop at the required number. Do not overshoot. Do not underturn.
      The dial must come to rest exactly at the value where the special click sounded.\n5)
      Release the lock. At that very moment the lock checks:\n\t- Did you stop at
      the correct number?\n\t- Was the last rotation in the correct direction?\n\n\tIf
      both conditions are met \u2014 the step counts. You move on to the next."
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page2.0
    value: Chapter V. The Price of a Mistake
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page2.1
    value: "\tA mistake is not only a wrong number. It is also a spin in the wrong
      direction. The mechanism does not forgive carelessness.\n\nIf you err:\n\t-
      The dial automatically makes two full revolutions and resets to position 0.\n\t-
      All combination progress is wiped.\n\t- You start over \u2014 from the first
      step, the first number, the first direction.\n\n\tA mistake at any step \u2014
      the first or the last \u2014 erases everything. The combination must be entered
      without a single error, from beginning to end."
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page2.2
    value: Chapter VI. Patience
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page2.3
    value: "\tA locksmith who rushes is a dead locksmith. The safe does not forgive
      haste. Every reset is not a defeat but a lesson. Each time you hear better.
      Each time your hand is steadier.\n\tAnd when at last the lock clicks and the
      door swings open \u2014 you will understand that it was not the safe you defeated.
      \n\tYou defeated yourself."
  - key: note.book_doctor_safe.page2.4
    value: Start from
  - key: note.book_morro_dairy.page0.0
    value: Morro's Diary
  - key: note.book_morro_dairy.page0.3
    value: "                            <b>Personal entry</b>\n\n\tI was thirteen
      when my mother had a stroke. For a week I sat by her bed, begging her to open
      her eyes.\n\tThe doctors said there was nothing more they could do. My father
      said: it is God's will.\n\tMother taught that everyone must face the final
      Trial alone.\n\tI became a doctor because I could not accept that."
  - key: note.book_morro_dairy.page1.1
    value: "                       <b>Working page</b>\n\n\tThe required substance
      is hidden within a heterogeneous suspension. First it must be purified mechanically
      \u2014 separate the binding fraction from the impurities.\n\tI have moved the
      further sequence into the laboratory\nprotocol."
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page0.0
    value: The Elder Futhark
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page0.12
    value: "Knowledge, speech, wisdom, \nthe divine word"
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page0.15
    value: "Journey, travel, movement, \ncontrol"
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page0.18
    value: Water, flow, intuition, emotions
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page0.3
    value: "Wealth, cattle, property, \nconfidence"
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page0.6
    value: "Strength, power, endurance, \nthe bull's energy"
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page0.9
    value: "Thorn, protection, overcoming, \ncaution"
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.11
    value: Need, constraint, necessity
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.14
    value: Ice, stagnation, pause, loneliness
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.17
    value: Harvest, cycle, reward for labor
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.2
    value: Gift, partnership, union, exchange
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.20
    value: Movement, progress, trust
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.23
    value: Mystery, fate, the unknown
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.26
    value: Protection, striving upward, support
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.29
    value: Sun, victory, clarity, energy
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.32
    value: Warrior, justice, victory, honor
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.35
    value: Birch, growth, renewal, spring
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.5
    value: Joy, harmony, success, positivity
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page1.8
    value: "Hail, destruction, cleansing, \nchange"
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page2.11
    value: "Fertility, completion, \na new beginning"
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page2.14
    value: Homeland, heritage, roots, traditions
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page2.17
    value: "Day, breakthrough, transformation, \ndawn"
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page2.2
    value: Yew, protection, endurance, choice
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page2.5
    value: Human, society, individual, reason
  - key: note.book_scandic_alphabet.page2.8
    value: "Torch, passion, creativity, \nenlightenment"
  - key: note.dairy_room1_1.page0.1
    value: "    <b>***</b>    \n    When the Darkness receded, I saw the face of
      a man in a white coat leaning over me. I lay in a white ward, surrounded by
      the breathing of machines. \n    Memory returned slowly. I remembered the locked
      warehouse, the ancient artifact \u2014 a mirror of the Qing era. Within it
      lies the Power and Karma of an entire dynasty. I had taken it upon myself to
      cleanse it. \n    Then everything swayed and began to collapse. \n    I shielded
      the mirror with my own body, and the glint of light within it blinded my eyes.
      \n    After that, the Darkness enveloped me\u2026 \n    But I was not asleep.
      There is no sleep there. For a long time I could not grasp that I was whole.
      Then I walked in silence and in darkness. \n    Something touched me. Cold
      and rough. It passed slowly along my body, as if checking that I existed. \n   
      Then came the sounds\u2026 A rustle\u2026 A whisper\u2026 Distant voices\u2026\n   
      I followed the sound. In the Darkness something moved along with me. Sometimes
      touching me. Guiding me\u2026 Calling me\u2026 It was the White Serpent. \n   
      And now I am conscious again.\n\n    ***\n    Doctor Morro told me about the
      accident and the coma that followed, in which I spent more than a month. And
      only now, in his clinic, they give me a new drug he calls the Frontier. He
      explained that although his drug can bring people out of a coma, the effect
      is temporary. Yet he is absolutely certain he can make it permanent. \n   
      For now, to strengthen the effect, he uses various kinds of stimulation: singing
      bowls, rubdowns. I asked him to add Huangqi, Danggui and Fuzi \u2014 herbs
      from my homeland \u2014 to the jade powder. \n    Having heard my account of
      what I endured, the doctor called it a Trial. \n    We both believe it can
      be passed.\n\n    ***\n    Six hours later I was in the Darkness again. It
      had been waiting for me. The White Serpent glided around me, as if in greeting.
      Rustles, hisses and whispers sounded from every side, and in them my name.
      \n    I followed the White Serpent, and at last I saw it \u2014 the Wheel of
      Samsara. \n"
  - key: note.dairy_room1_2.page0.1
    value: "\t\n    <b>***</b>\n\u2026The doctor says that with each injection I
      spend more and more time conscious. Though each time it takes me longer to
      wake. \n\n    <b>***</b>\n\u2026More than 9 hours have passed since the last
      injection\u2026 and already I cannot wait for the Darkness to come again. \n   
      More and more I feel that my place is not here.\n    Morro tries to fight this
      effect, but I am not sure I want him to\u2026 \n    I look at the shards of
      glass on the floor and understand: all I need is to complete the Circle!"
  - key: note.dairy_room2_1.page0.1
    value: "    <b>December 10</b>\n    In my parents' workshop near Kyoto there
      stood shelves of cracked bowls. Mother trusted me to wash the dust from them.
      I knew they had to be held carefully, with both hands. That is how they hold
      me now.\n    At twenty-five I came to New York with my paintings. Now I have
      three galleries, yet sometimes the only way I can ask for the right paint is
      by the number on the tube.\n    The critics write that with my illness I discovered
      new colors. I did not discover them. I lost the old ones.\n\n    <b>December
      12</b>\n    The doctor says that in sleep my brain works correctly. Which means
      somewhere I still know how to see the sky.\n    I agreed to the Frontier. I
      asked for paper: I want to write down what happens while I am gone."
  - key: note.dairy_room2_1.page0.2
    value: "     ****\n    Clara said these drawings are mine. But I cannot draw
      like that.\n    I will not paint over the sky yet. I have no blue crayon\u2026\n\n   
      \u2013 When is Mommy coming?\n"
  - key: note.dairy_room2_1.page0.3
    value: "    <b>December 15</b>\n    I read the previous entry. Mother died many
      years ago. I must ask Clara not to tell the girl. There is no need to upset
      her.\n \n\u2026I told Clara about Cirque du Soleil. She listened so intently
      that I ordered her a ticket to the February premiere. It is nice to do something
      here, just once, that has nothing to do with illness.\n\n    <b>December 18</b>\n   
      I dreamed of the valley again. I used to paint that place: there was a garden
      there. Now beings walk there for which I have no names.\n    I draw them immediately
      upon waking. I had hoped to leave them in my paintings. Today in the dream
      I recognized one of them: yesterday I did not have time to finish its hands.\n   
      It showed me where they should be.\n"
  - key: note.dairy_room2_2.page0.1
    value: '                                                                   From
      the Diary of Chiwa Sabi

'
  - key: note.dairy_room2_2.page0.2
    value: "    <b>***</b>\n    I painted over its eyes.\n    It is still watching."
  - key: note.dairy_room2_2.page0.3
    value: "    <b>December 19</b>\n    Clara begs me to stop. But today I distinguished
      all four rays. After so many months of mistakes \u2014 not one. I cannot leave
      it now.\n    The paintings change in height and light. I repeat this so as
      not to forget! \n    I will write it on the canvas too, just in case!\n"
  - key: note.dairy_room2_2.page0.4
    value: "    <b>***</b>\n    Everything is arranged correctly. The signs are visible.\n   
      \u2013 I have decided, Doctor!\n    Why does the garden not return?\n"
  - key: note.dairy_room2_2.page0.5
    value: "    <b>***</b>\n    There is a red star on the wall. One must look at
      it and hold the medallion.\n    I am fifty-four years old. My name is Chiwa
      Sabi\u2026\n"
  - key: note.dairy_room2_2.page0.6
    value: "    <b>***</b>\n    Mother, I don't want to paint anymore!.."
  - key: note.dairy_room3_1.page0.1
    value: "    <b>***</b>\n    \u2026First I heard numbers.\n\n    \u2014 2, 3,
      5, 7, 11, 13,\u2026\n\n    Someone was leaving pauses between them. Waiting
      for me.\n\n    \u2014 \u202617, 19, 23,...\n\n    I opened my eyes. The man
      by the bed introduced himself as Doctor Morro. I asked him to continue. While
      he counted, I studied the edge of the blanket, the doorway, my own hands.\n\n   
      <b>***</b>\n    \u2026While I was not here, I walked down a corridor.\n   
      It is warm there. The light is such as on a summer day beyond the windows,
      though I do not remember the windows themselves. Golden motes drift slowly
      in circles. One can sit by the wall, close one's eyes. For the first time in
      many years I need not count anything for it to grow quiet.\n    And yet \u2014
      I do not sit down. I feel that if I settle in comfortably, I will forget why
      I stood up.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n    The second time, the prime numbers did not
      help. I could hear the doctor's voice but could not reach it. Then he began
      a different sequence.\n\n    1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8\u2026\n\n    I knew what came
      next. I clung to that knowledge\u2026\n    Afterwards we talked for a long
      time. The doctor is pleased. \n    I did not tell him that just before I woke,
      a door slammed somewhere in the corridor. Not the one I was walking toward.
      Behind me.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n    Now It is there. I did not see It enter.
      There was simply a chill between my shoulder blades. The light is still warm.
      But that only makes it worse.\n    I walk toward the door. I count my steps.
      The distance does not shrink.\n    I must not walk at random! This place must
      have a pattern."
  - key: note.dairy_room3_2.page0.1
    value: "    \n    <b>The Third Awakening</b>\n    First Fibonacci. Then doubling:
      \n\n    1, 2, 4, 8\u2026 \n\n    It grew easier for a while.\n    Now I hear
      only the gaps between numbers. Within them \u2014 footsteps.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n   
      I draw eyes. I try to recreate the path of the golden motes. They did not move
      at random. A spiral unfolded from the center, and each turn\u2026\n    Do not
      look back.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n    I remember how pleasant it was to be there\u2026\n   
      That is more frightening than the footsteps\u2026\n\n    <b>***</b>\n    The
      doctor promises to wake me once more.\n    I must finish the calculation before
      I find myself in the corridor again.\n    Because I still want to get out of
      there.\n    But no longer all the time."
  - key: note.dairy_room4_1.page0.1
    value: "    \n    <b>***</b>\n    I was eight when my father first set me at
      the lathe. My legs did not reach the floor. I remember his fingers over mine:
      \u201CDon't press. Feel where it resists.\u201D\n    Funny. He understood that
      with metal.\n    I pulled the workshop out of debt and moved it to new premises.
      The queue to us stretched months ahead. But father kept asking when our own
      watches would come out. I answered: soon. And again stayed to work through
      the night.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n    Morro took away the watches. He said there
      is no hurry here.\n    At first I was angry. Then I learned to sit on the veranda
      doing nothing. Just to sit. It turns out I had never tried that before.\n   
      And now they say that yesterday I sat for several hours without answering questions.
      For me, a single instant had passed. A nurse stood by the door \u2014 and was
      suddenly beside me, holding my shoulder.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n    It was the
      ticking that brought me back.\n    Before even opening my eyes I heard it:
      a pendulum, a heavy stride. Almost like grandfather's mantel clocks. At once
      I wanted to check the mechanism.\n    After the injection, my hands obey me
      again. The doctor brought instruments.\n    I came here because I could no
      longer bear to hear the clocks. Now I cannot wake without them.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n   
      There, I walk down the corridor all the time. It turns right. A turn, another
      turn, never a straight stretch. It is like a mainspring.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n   
      They brought more watches. One is missing.\n    I am trying to set a single
      time. I need to determine what it should be.\n    They took me away from the
      workshop. The work remained the same."
  - key: note.dairy_room4_2.page0.1
    value: "\t\n    <b>***</b>\n    Today a door appeared in the corridor. Beyond
      it there was light. \n    I stepped through \u2014 and opened my eyes in the
      ward.\n    I thought: it worked. But then I was back on the same turn.\n   
      Morro says I created the door myself. The true way out is something else. I
      wanted to ask how he knows, but he was already looking at the instruments.\n\n   
      <b>***</b>\n    Father taught: if the mechanism will not go, do not wind it
      harder. Look for what is blocking it.\n    I wanted to tell the doctor. He
      was in a hurry.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n    I need time. \n    Not my father. \n   
      Not the workshop.\n    Me.\n"
  - key: note.dairy_room5_1.page0.1
    value: "    \n    <b>***</b>\n   At home not even an armchair could be moved.
      That is why I always wanted to create places where a person chooses their own
      path.\n\u2026I built my first labyrinth with Christian. I trusted him with
      everything. After his betrayal I was left at a dead end: a physical and a psychological
      one.\n   But I got out. Later I opened a center. And I kept repeating: \u201CThere
      is no hurry \u2014 you can choose your own path.\u201D\n\n   <b>***</b>\n  
      I remember the headlights of a truck. Then \u2014 walls.\n   Stone gives way
      to the marble of our house, around the turn the corn rustles. In the mirrors
      stands Christian. I know what he will say.\n   These are places from my past.
      I ought to know the way.\n   But all the roads are joined wrongly.\n\n   <b>***</b>\n  
      I got out\u2026 Someone was holding my palm. I tried to draw a sign of protection
      on it. It came out as the Algiz rune.\n   Morro said he would help me. While
      he spoke, I could still feel the stone floor under my bare feet.\n\n   <b>***</b>\n  
      On the nightstand lie a red ball of thread and a bonsai. A reminder\nfrom Mark,
      my faithful assistant. Without him I would not have managed\u2026\n   I ran
      a finger along the bark, and among the walls a root appeared. That means\nI
      can chart a new path.\n   I draw it. From Odal \u2014 the home where it all
      began \u2014 to Gebo. Trust must be confirmed twice: to accept help from outside
      and to give strength\nin return. The final goal is Algiz, the sign of healing
      and protection. Wunjo \u2014\nmy former carefree joy \u2014 will remain only
      a warm\nglint at the edge.\n   The reversed sign is to be left outside. Do
      not weave it in again.\n\n   <b>***</b>\n   Today I saw the light.\n   The
      doctor asked how far away it was. I wanted to explain: here distance means
      nothing. One must find the turn.\n   He repeated the question again and again.
      His voice made me forget who I was and where I was going.\n"
  - key: note.dairy_room5_2.page0.1
    value: "\t\n    <b>***</b>\n    \u2026Among the gray walls, footsteps can be
      heard. Someone was walking toward me.\n    I called out.\n    The footsteps
      stopped.\n    Morro's voice was very close, but it came from above.\n\n   
      <b>***</b>\n    He says there is little time.\n    I know. But if you hurry,
      all the passages become alike.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n    The thread is still with
      me. I pinch it between my fingers.\n    Someone is pulling from the other end.\n   
      I wanted to scream: \n\n    \u2013 Don't pull! It has caught on a corner. Slacken
      it a little so I can free it.\n\n    But my voice was lost in the encroaching
      darkness.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n    The light is still visible\u2026\n    Please,
      do not pull so hard\u2026\n\n"
  - key: note.dairy_room6_1.page0.1
    value: "\t\n    <b>***</b>\n    First came back the chirring. Then \u2014 the
      feel of silk under my fingers. I decided I had fallen asleep by the terrariums.
      I need to check the humidity and prepare my notes for the lecture.\n    I could
      not get up. Even my hands obey me with difficulty.\n    Morro said I lay in
      a coma for a month. I wonder who fed my insects all that time.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n   
      As a child I brought tadpoles from the abandoned docks. I waited for their
      legs to appear. It seemed to me that one day something I lacked would appear
      for me too.\n    Then came the burned laboratory, the surgeries, years without
      a family. I restored the research and defended my dissertation. I learned to
      say my name calmly before a full auditorium.\n    I liked my life.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n   
      Today we talked for a long time. Morro asked about rebirth. I tried to explain:
      the stillness of the chrysalis is deceptive. Work goes on inside.\n    He was
      very glad.\n    Perhaps I chose the wrong example.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n    There,
      I lie in a thin shell. Warm light passes through it. Outside, the forest is
      loud.\n    At first the tightness frightened me. Then I understood: no one
      can touch me. One can stop defending oneself. Simply wait.\n    But more and
      more I want to see what is outside."
  - key: note.dairy_room6_2.page0.1
    value: "\t\n    <b>***</b>\n    After the new injection the shell grew tighter.
      I cannot straighten my arms.\n    But now I can see the door. It is far away,
      beyond a translucent wall. Beneath it runs a strip of light.\n    The doctor
      asks whether I can reach it.\n    I have not even gotten out of here yet.\n\n   
      <b>***</b> \n    The forest has grown louder. The same splash, chirr, pause.\n   
      Something can be heard beyond the door too.\n    It does not repeat.\n\n   
      <b>***</b>\n    Today, for the first time, I felt a wing at my back.\n    I
      know I do not have one \u2014 I checked with my hand, as far as I could reach.\n   
      But the feeling remained.\n\n    <b>***</b>\n    Morro promises to help me
      out.\n    I must ask: out to where.\n    Why did I never ask before?\n\n   
      <b>***</b>\n    I still want to open the door.\n    Only let someone stay on
      this side of it."
  - key: note.ftuecontrols.page0.0
    value: Controls
  - key: note.ftuecontrols.page0.10
    value: Toggle flashlight
  - key: note.ftuecontrols.page0.12
    value: Journal
  - key: note.ftuecontrols.page0.14
    value: Inventory
